# openssl -- cargo links against it, so rustc cannot be packaged without it.
#
# 3.5 LTS, not 4.0, and the difference is the difference between a distribution
# that can speak TLS and one that cannot.
#
# OpenSSL 4.0 made the ASN1_* string types opaque. Python 3.13's _ssl and curl
# 8.13 both reach directly into them, so both failed to compile identically:
#
#   invalid use of incomplete typedef 'ASN1_OCTET_STRING'
#   invalid use of incomplete typedef 'ASN1_BIT_STRING'
#
# Two independent consumers failing the same way is a fact about the version,
# not about them. The alternative to repinning was a parallel compat package
# for everything that cannot build against 4.0, which is what the one
# mainstream distribution shipping 4.0 has had to do.
#
# Nothing here required 4.0. rust is the only package in the tree that links
# OpenSSL at all, and the crate it links through -- openssl-sys, vendored in the
# rust tarball -- says in its own build script that it "only works with OpenSSL
# 1.1.0, 1.1.1 and 3.x". Its version ladder in build/cfgs.rs is cumulative
# rather than a floor, with an explicit ossl350 tier: 3.5 is a first-class
# configuration there, and ossl400 is a capability flag it simply does not set.
# The 4.0 pin was reactive -- cargo failed to find libssl.so.4 and the recipe
# was amended to name whatever was installed.
#
# 3.5.7 is the newest patch release of the 3.5 LTS series, which is what
# everything outside Fedora is on.
#
# Not an LFS base package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/openssl/openssl/releases/download/openssl-3.5.7/openssl-3.5.7.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=a8c0d28a529ca480f9f36cf5792e2cd21984552a3c8e4aa11a24aa31aeac98e8
SRC_DIR=openssl-3.5.7
